        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Context</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Fable 5.1 / Mythos 5.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$10</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$50</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold text-blue-700">$0.25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1M</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Opus 5 / 4.8 / 4.7 / 4.6 / 4.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.50</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1M</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Sonnet 5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$15</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.30</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1M</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Haiku 4.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.10</td><td class="px-4 py-2">200K</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The interesting comparison is Fable 5.1 against Opus 5. Opus 5 is half the price on input ($5 vs $10) and half the price on output ($25 vs $50), and its cache-read tier is double ($0.50 vs $0.25). For workloads that lean heavy on cache reuse — Claude Code sessions, long agent runs, multi-turn RAG — Fable 5.1 wins on effective cost even at the higher headline rate. For workloads that burn through raw input and output tokens without significant cache reuse — short single-turn API calls, one-shot completions — Opus 5 is the cheaper option. Anthropic's own guidance now says to use Fable 5.1 for "demanding reasoning and long-horizon agentic work, or when your evals on Claude Opus 5 at higher effort still fall short."</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enterprise Frontier Safeguards (EFS)</h2>
  <p>EFS is the most operationally important change in the Fable 5.1 launch for any team that processes customer data through Claude. The architecture has three properties:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-gray-700">
    <li><strong>Customer-controlled storage.</strong> Request and response data lives in cloud infrastructure controlled by the customer, not Anthropic. Anthropic never sees the plaintext prompt or completion on disk — the streamed inference traffic is processed and discarded.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero-data-retention equivalence.</strong> Because Anthropic does not persist the data, the operational privacy story is identical to running Claude under a zero-data-retention (ZDR) contract. Enterprise legal and compliance teams can sign off on Fable 5.1 with the same paperwork they already use for ZDR.</li>
    <li><strong>Classifier pipeline still works.</strong> Anthropic's safety classifiers run on the streamed data without persisting it, which means EFS gets the same safety quality as a default Anthropic deployment. The 60% cyber false-positive reduction announced alongside Fable 5.1 applies to EFS customers as well.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The rollout is phased. Anthropic says EFS becomes available "in phases, beginning later this fall" — so as of September 2, 2026 EFS is not yet generally available. Until then, eligible customers can opt into zero-data-retention mode for Fable 5.1 directly through the Anthropic console, which gives the same privacy posture with less control over where the data lives during processing. Anthropic has not published EFS-specific pricing as of this writing; the cost model is expected to be a per-token surcharge on top of the base Fable 5.1 rate.</p>
  <p>For teams that already pay for Claude under a ZDR contract, EFS is an incremental improvement: the privacy story stays the same, but you also get explicit storage control for compliance audits. For teams running Claude under the default data-retention terms (where Anthropic may store prompts for up to 30 days for abuse monitoring), EFS is the first tier that lets you ship customer data through Claude without that exposure window.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Benchmarks: Where Fable 5.1 Beats Fable 5</h2>
  <p>Anthropic published six head-to-head benchmark comparisons in the launch post, with the explicit caveat that the public-leaderboard versions of two benchmarks (Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1 and Terminal-Bench 4.0) sit within the standard-error window of Anthropic's internal harness. The headline numbers:</p>
