<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">API &amp; Developer Experience</h2>
  <p>The Fable 5.1 API is a drop-in replacement for Fable 5 on the same Anthropic Messages endpoint. The minimum migration is a model-ID swap:</p>
  <pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>import anthropic

client = anthropic.Anthropic()  # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY

# Before (Fable 5)
resp = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-fable-5-20250901",
    max_tokens=2048,
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Find the bug in this C++ snippet."}],
)

# After (Fable 5.1 — same SDK, same endpoint, different model ID)
resp = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-fable-5-1",
    max_tokens=2048,
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Find the bug in this C++ snippet."}],
)</code></pre>
  <p>The new effort parameter is the only meaningful API surface change. Effort is an integer enum (low=1, medium=2, high=3, xhigh=4, max=5) that controls how much compute Fable 5.1 spends per request. Higher effort costs more tokens because the model thinks longer, but produces better answers on hard reasoning tasks. The cost delta on a per-request basis can be 3-10× between low and max effort:</p>
  <pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>resp = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-fable-5-1",
    max_tokens=8192,
    effort="xhigh",  # or "max" for the deepest reasoning
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Diagnose this 3AM production outage from the stack trace."}],
)</code></pre>
  <p>Other surface areas that did not change:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-gray-700">
    <li><strong>1M-token context window</strong> (same as Opus 5 and Sonnet 5; double Haiku 4.5's 200K).</li>
    <li><strong>128K max output tokens</strong> per response (same as Opus 5 and Sonnet 5).</li>
    <li><strong>Reliable knowledge cutoff June 2026</strong> (the model can answer confidently about events through ~June 2026; later events need explicit context).</li>
    <li><strong>Adaptive thinking</strong> (extended thinking variant available; always on at higher effort levels).</li>
    <li><strong>Tools / function calling / JSON mode</strong> — same schema as Fable 5.</li>
    <li><strong>Vision input</strong> — text and image input on all current Claude models.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One operational gotcha worth noting: prompt caches built on Fable 5 are not portable to Fable 5.1. Cache keys are model-scoped, so the Fable 5.1 cutover requires a re-warm cycle. In a long-running Claude Code session that has accumulated 30 minutes of cached context, expect 5-30 minutes of full-price input tokens after the swap before the new cache stabilizes. Plan the cutover for low-traffic windows if your prompt cache is a significant cost component.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cache Read Pricing: Why It Matters</h2>
  <p>For most teams using Claude for production workloads, the 75% cache-read cut is the only number that affects the monthly bill. Anthropic's own cost-decomposition chart shows that in a "typical" Claude Code session, cache reads make up roughly half of the total spend. In an agent that re-uses a large accumulated context (the kind of agent Fable 5.1 is built for), cache reads can exceed 70% of the total. Cutting that tier from $1.00 to $0.25 therefore has an outsized effect on total cost:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-gray-700">
    <li><strong>Typical Claude Code session</strong> — 25% lower effective cost. A team spending $10,000/month on Fable 5 cache reads drops to $2,500 on Fable 5.1, a $7,500/month saving.</li>
    <li><strong>Long-running research / coding agent</strong> — 45% lower effective cost. Anthropic's example is a multi-hour agent that re-uses a 600K-token system prompt plus accumulated tool results; on Fable 5 the cache reads alone exceeded the input token cost; on Fable 5.1 they are 75% cheaper.</li>
    <li><strong>RAG pipelines</strong> — variable. A naive RAG setup that re-sends the retrieved context on every call gets minimal cache benefit. A setup that maintains a stable system prompt with the retrieved context appended sees the same 25-45% savings.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-turn completions</strong> — no cache benefit. The headline rate ($10/$50) is unchanged; Fable 5.1 costs the same as Fable 5 for one-shot API calls. Use Opus 5 or Sonnet 5 for these.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The economics make Fable 5.1 the obvious choice for any workload that benefits from prompt caching, and Opus 5 / Sonnet 5 the obvious choice for workloads that don't. There is no Fable 5.1 feature that is gated behind higher spend — all the new capabilities (better agentic coding, EFS, biology research on Mythos 5.1) ship at the same per-token cost as Fable 5.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Edge Latency, China Access &amp; Where to Run Fable 5.1</h2>
  <p>Anthropic serves Fable 5.1 from the same US-based infrastructure as the rest of the Claude lineup. There is no Fable 5.1-specific edge node; latency and regional availability match Opus 5. North America customers typically see 200-400 ms time-to-first-token on first-token streaming, with ~600-900 ms TTFT for the European and Asia-Pacific regions depending on routing. The 1M-token context window makes TTFT noticeably higher on cold-start than smaller-context models because the entire 1M-token attention prefill runs before the first token streams.</p>
  <p>Mainland China is the explicit gap. Anthropic does not operate any inference region in mainland China, and the export-control suspension for foreign nationals is still in place. Outbound traffic from CN to api.anthropic.com is regularly throttled by the GFW, so even API calls from a Chinese IP typically see 1.5-3 second TTFT or intermittent connection failures. For end-user applications serving CN traffic the recommended path is a domestic provider (Zhipu GLM, Alibaba Qwen on Bailian, ByteDance Doubao on Volcengine, DeepSeek) — reserve Fable 5.1 for non-CN workloads.</p>
  <p>If you want Claude-style behavior on a CN-routable endpoint without managing a domestic provider relationship, OpenRouter exposes Fable 5.1 via its OpenAI-compatible routing layer. Other aggregators (FreeModel, Together AI, Fireworks AI) typically add Fable 5.1 within 7-14 days of a public Anthropic release. For self-hosted, there is no open-weights Fable 5.1 — Anthropic continues to ship the model only via its hosted API.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fable 5.1 vs Opus 5 vs Sonnet 5 vs GPT-5.6</h2>
  <p>Four models are now competing for the "best general-purpose Anthropic API" slot. Here is how they stack up as of September 2, 2026:</p>
  <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 my-4 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
      <tr>
        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Model</th>
        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Input $/MTok</th>
        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Output $/MTok</th>
        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Cache read $/MTok</th>
        <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Best for</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Claude Fable 5.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$10</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$50</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold text-blue-700">$0.25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Long-horizon agentic work, demanding reasoning, cache-heavy Claude Code sessions</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Claude Opus 5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.50</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Complex enterprise coding, balanced cost/quality, fallback when Fable 5.1 is too expensive</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Claude Sonnet 5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$15</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.30</td><td class="px-4 py-2">High-volume production, default Claude API tier, near-frontier quality at half the price</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Claude Haiku 4.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.10</td><td class="px-4 py-2">High-throughput classification, subagents, 200K context only</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">GPT-5.6 (OpenAI)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$0.50 (cached input)</td><td class="px-4 py-2">OpenAI ecosystem, Cursor integrations, parallel GPT-OSS comparison</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The Fable 5.1 vs GPT-5.6 comparison is the harder one. Both models charge the same base rate ($10/$50 for Fable 5.1 vs $5/$25 for GPT-5.6) and both have 1M-token context windows. Fable 5.1 wins on cache-heavy workloads (75% cheaper cache reads) and on Anthropic's agentic-coding benchmarks (CursorBench, Terminal-Bench-Science). GPT-5.6 wins on raw input/output cost and on the OpenAI ecosystem (Cursor integration, OpenAI AgentKit, fine-tuning access on related tiers). For teams already on Anthropic, Fable 5.1 is the obvious upgrade; for teams on OpenAI, the migration tax is real but the cost savings on cache-heavy work may justify it.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Should Migrate to Fable 5.1</h2>
  <p>Three profiles should move to Fable 5.1 immediately:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-gray-700">
    <li><strong>Production Claude Code / Cursor / Windsurf users</strong> — agentic IDE workflows benefit the most from the 75% cache-read cut. A team spending $20,000/month on Fable 5 cache reads drops to $5,000/month on Fable 5.1, and the agentic-coding benchmarks (Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1, Terminal-Bench 4.0, CursorBench 3.2.0) show Fable 5.1 out-performing Fable 5 across the suite.</li>
    <li><strong>Enterprise customers with data-residency requirements</strong> — EFS is the first Anthropic tier that delivers zero-data-retention equivalent privacy without sacrificing safety-classifier coverage. Teams shipping customer PII, healthcare data (HIPAA), or financial records through Claude should move to Fable 5.1 with EFS once the fall 2026 rollout reaches their account.</li>
    <li><strong>Bio / chem / cybersecurity research teams (US only at launch)</strong> — Mythos 5.1's biology and cyber access programs are the first Anthropic SKU that ships biology-research capability to vetted teams. International expansion will follow the US-government-coordinated rollout, so non-US teams should plan for a 6-12 month wait.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Three profiles should hold off:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-gray-700">
    <li><strong>Single-turn / one-shot completion workloads</strong> — no cache benefit, no agentic-coding benefit. Opus 5 at half the headline rate is the better choice.</li>
    <li><strong>Mainland-China end-user applications</strong> — Anthropic still does not serve inference from any in-China region. Use a domestic provider (Zhipu GLM, Alibaba Qwen, DeepSeek) or an aggregator with CN routing.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost-sensitive high-volume classification</strong> — Sonnet 5 at $3/$15 or Haiku 4.5 at $1/$5 deliver near-frontier quality at a fraction of the Fable 5.1 rate. Reserve Fable 5.1 for the workloads that need its specific strengths.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Verdict</h2>
  <p>Claude Fable 5.1 on September 2, 2026 is the default Anthropic model for any workload that benefits from prompt caching. The 75% cache-read cut is the headline pricing change, but the operationally important shift is Enterprise Frontier Safeguards — for the first time, enterprise customers can route Claude traffic through a tier that delivers zero-data-retention-equivalent privacy without losing safety classifier coverage. On agentic-coding benchmarks (Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1, CursorBench 3.2.0, AutomationBench) Fable 5.1 at max effort substantially outperforms both Fable 5 and Opus 5; on reasoning benchmarks (Humanity's Last Exam, GDPval-AA v2) it sits roughly equal to Opus 5 at max effort. The migration cost from Fable 5 is a model-ID swap plus a 5-30 minute cache re-warm — the cheapest meaningful upgrade Anthropic has shipped in 2026. Mythos 5.1 is the same model with tighter safeguards; it is the right pick for vetted biology / cyber research groups but irrelevant to most production workloads. For teams on Opus 5 the upgrade is a careful cost-vs-quality evaluation: Fable 5.1 wins on cache-heavy agentic work but loses on raw input/output cost; for single-turn or non-cached workloads, Opus 5 at half the rate remains the better choice.</p>
</section>
`;
